>[]($block.attrs("author-note"))
> Oh boy oh boy it's time for me to do bullets again! This month I started being gainfully
> employed once more, so it is... slim pickings to say the least.

# Stuff I Did
## Ran
* I said in February that I was aiming to run my 10k PR at a race this month, and I am
very proud to say that I did! I ran the Zandvoort Circuit run and in addition to setting a
10k PR of 45:55 I also came in 129th place out of almost 5200, which I am very proud of.
* I've also started working more seriously on my running form this month. I'm a pretty
chronic over-strider and especially with how rapidly my pace has improved over the last
few months I'm starting to really feel it in my knees. I'm making slow progress, but I
know it's gonna take me a few months to get it where I want it (just in time for my main
goal race of the year hopefully).

## Played
I played basically nothing this month, sadly. I did pick up a bunch of new games (curse
you Steam spring sale), and I've at least spent a few minutes with each of them, but with
starting a job again and having a ton of other stuff on my calendar this month I just
never found the time to sit down and get into anything fully. I definitely want to take
another good chunk out of my backlog in April though, starting with *Hi-Fi Rush* and
*Cairn*.

## Read
### Books
Similar to my game time taking a hit, so did my reading time this month, being limited to
basically only before bed. I got through two books:
* [The Tainted Cup](https://hardcover.app/books/the-tainted-cup) - Had been on my "to
read" stack for quite a while. I thought it was good, the world is super interesting and I
always enjoy a good detective story. It didn't grab me like a lot of other things, but I'm
sure I'll be back to finish the trilogy at some point in the future (it just may be a
while).
* [Iron Gold](https://hardcover.app/books/iron-gold) - I could not help myself from
returning to the Red Rising universe. In many ways Iron Gold feels similar to the original
first book for me: not bad in its own right but very much a setup book for the good stuff
later on. I'm more than halfway through Dark Age now and can't put it down.

### Not Books
Only one thing here this go around, which is [You don’t have to if you don’t want to]( https://www.scottsmitelli.com/articles/you-dont-have-to/)
by Scott Smitelli, who is one of my favorite technical writers (maybe because he's just a
really good writer). It is (surprise surprise) about LLMs for software engineering, and it
really resonates with a lot of my experience, especially now that I've joined a company
that is much more bullish on the technology than I am.

